  "query_preview": "..\n\nStrong surface high pressure is in place over Southeast Michigan \nthis morning with negligible surface flow. The lack of any \nadvections to this point has allowed remnant Great Lakes moisture to \nremain trapped below an inversion with a base at 1.0 to 2.0 kft agl. \nAs a result, widespread stratus/stratocumulus has been able to hold \non from the Tri Cities and Flint to the southeast. Forecast \nsoundings strongly suggest the support for cloud will lessen during \nthe daylight hours as rising boundary layer heights will allow cloud \nto mix out. Did extend sky fraction in the grids to the mid to late \nmorning hours.\n\nGeopotential thickness will increase substantially over Southeast \nMichigan this week as shortwave ridging today gives way to long \nwavelength omega blocking in quick succession. Significant column \nwarming will occur with H5 heights pushing 582 dam already by \nTuesday evening. 850mb temperatures will surge to +10C by Wednesday \nevening. High temperatures are expected to be in the middle 50s \nTuesday and Wednesday (5-8 degrees above normal) and lower 60s \nThursday through Saturday(10-15 degrees above normal). Southwest \nwinds will remain seasonally modest throughout much of the week with \ngradient possibly supporting 20 to 30 mph by the end of the week. \nThe polar westerly branch is forecasted to push southward by this \nupcoming weekend. Difficult to offer much timing of precipitation, \nbut there has been consistency in frontal zone through the region on \nSunday.",
